{{- /*
	resolve_object writes how an object reaching a file answers what JSON it
	becomes. It cannot be dumped plainly: a file has to leave the body for a part
	of its own, so the dump is told to leave every such field out and each one is
	put back as whatever points at its part.

	It is one template and not two, where the Go target needs a second for an
	object a union tells apart. There the discriminator is written as the object
	marshals itself, and the rewrite strips that marshaller away, so the value has
	to be written again by hand. Here the discriminator is a declared field, so the
	dump writes it like any other.

	It is a shape, not an entity: any view offering Name and Files renders through
	it.
*/}}
{{- define "resolve_object"}}
    def _resolve(self, sink: _FileSink) -> dict[str, Any]:
        data = _dump(self{{range .Files}}, "{{.Name}}"{{end}})
{{- range .Files}}
{{- render .Template .}}
{{- end}}
        return data
{{- end}}

{{- /*
	resolve_dump writes the same answer for an object a union reaching a file
	admits but which holds no file of its own. It has nothing to hand over, so
	what it becomes is what the dump says, and the sink goes untouched.

	An object can be admitted by two such unions at once, which is why the answer
	is written here, once, where the object is declared rather than where a union
	lists it.
*/}}
{{- define "resolve_dump"}}
    def _resolve(self, sink: _FileSink) -> dict[str, Any]:
        return _dump(self)
{{- end}}
